# `sourceGenerators` runs every generator on every evaluation of `compile`, so a generator that
# rewrites its output when nothing changed invalidates the compile that follows it, on every
# keystroke of a watch loop. Nothing guarded that until this file existed. See build.sbt here for
# what these steps can and cannot tell apart.

# A first run writes the table, with the handwritten row and the derived line both in it.
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertRoutesExist
> assertTableHasIndex
> assertTableHasWidget
> recordRoutes

# Nothing changed, so nothing may move.
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertRoutesUntouched

# A file outside `app/` carrying neither a route nor a model. The whole source tree is a hashed
# input, so the cache misses and the body does run; the table must still not move. This is the step
# that guards `writeIfChanged`, and the only one where the two defences disagree.
$ copy-file changes/Note.scala src/main/scala/Note.scala
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertRoutesUntouched

# A new file under `app/` must reach the table.
$ copy-file changes/Show.scala src/main/scala/app/widgets/_id/Show.scala
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertRoutesRewritten
> assertTableHasShow
> recordRoutes

# Deleting the output regenerates it, because the cache tracks the output as well as the inputs.
# Without that, a `clean` would leave a project unable to compile until a source was touched.
> deleteRoutes
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertRoutesExist
> assertTableHasShow
> recordRoutes

# Emptying it repairs it, because the output is tracked by content hash rather than by existence,
# so a hand-edited table is not left standing.
> truncateRoutes
> Compile/eezoGenerateRoutes
> assertTableHasShow
